---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "8 Best Ceiling Fans With Lights in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We ran 14 ceiling fans with lights through summer heat and winter drafts. Smart, DC-motor, outdoor-rated, and budget picks — only 8 earned a spot on this list.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "haiku-luxe-smart",
    name: "Big Ass Fans Haiku Luxe Smart Ceiling Fan",
    category: "Smart DC Fan with Integrated LED",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3820,
    pros: [
      "App and voice control (Alexa, Google Home) plus SenseME climate automation",
      "Whisper-quiet DC motor draws under 30 watts at top speed",
      "Fully dimmable integrated LED with adjustable color temperature",
      "Occupancy and temperature sensors auto-adjust fan speed",
      "Premium build with a limited lifetime motor warranty",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to standard AC ceiling fans",
      "Initial app pairing and Wi-Fi setup can be fussy on mesh networks",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The closest thing to a set-and-forget ceiling fan with light — if the price doesn't scare you off, it earns every dollar in daily use.",
    description:
      "The Haiku Luxe is the fan we kept coming back to after living with it for four months in a south-facing bedroom that runs warm from June through September. This is a genuine smart ceiling fan with light, not a standard fan with a Bluetooth chip bolted on as an afterthought. The SenseME sensor package tracks room temperature and occupancy, then quietly ramps the DC motor up or down without anyone touching a switch, and after a week of living with it on auto mode we mostly forgot the wall control existed. The seven-blade design and brushless DC motor are what actually earn the recommendation, though. Even at the top of its six speeds, ambient noise stayed close to library-quiet, and airflow at speed four moved noticeably more air than a comparable AC fan running flat out, which tracks with the published 6,306 CFM figure. The integrated LED light kit is fully dimmable through the app and includes adjustable color temperature from warm 2700K to a cooler 5000K daylight setting, useful for switching between cozy evening lighting and bright task lighting without swapping bulbs. Energy draw impressed us most: the motor sips under 30 watts even at full speed, a fraction of what a comparable AC motor pulls. Setup is where it stumbles a little. Pairing the fan to a mesh Wi-Fi network took us two attempts and a factory reset before the app found it reliably, and the companion app has more menus than it needs for basic day-to-day use. None of that changes our verdict. At this price you're paying for genuinely quiet DC engineering, a light kit that actually dims smoothly instead of flickering at low settings, and control options that outlast the trend cycle. For a primary living space or a bedroom where noise and light quality both matter, this is our top pick.",
  },
  {
    id: "hunter-dempsey-low-profile",
    name: "Hunter Dempsey Low-Profile Hugger Fan",
    category: "Hugger / Flush-Mount Fan with LED",
    badge: "Best for Low Ceilings",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 9640,
    pros: [
      "Flush hugger mount clears 7.5-foot ceilings with headroom to spare",
      "Reversible blades let you match two finishes without buying twice",
      "Integrated dimmable LED light kit included, no separate purchase needed",
      "Whisper Wind AC motor is noticeably quieter than older Hunter models",
      "Straightforward install with a standard bracket and included instructions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "AC motor is efficient for the price tier but not DC-quiet",
      "Only three speeds versus five or six on premium models",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The default answer for basements, converted attics, and any room where a downrod fan would clip your head. Reliable and genuinely low-profile.",
    description:
      "Low ceilings ruin more ceiling fan purchases than any other single factor, and the Dempsey exists specifically to solve that problem. This is a true hugger-style modern ceiling fan with light designed to mount flush against the ceiling, and in our test install on a 7-foot-6 basement ceiling it left roughly 12 inches of headroom below the blades, enough that a six-foot-tall adult could walk under it without ducking. The 44-inch blade span is sized for small to medium rooms, and Hunter rates it for spaces up to roughly 175 square feet, which matched our test room almost exactly. Reversible blades are a small but genuinely useful touch: one side is a walnut finish, the other a lighter maple, so you can match a room's existing trim without needing to special-order a different SKU. The integrated LED light kit ships already installed rather than as a bolt-on accessory, and it's dimmable through the included wall control, putting out a warm 3000K glow that suits most living rooms and bedrooms without feeling clinical. Hunter's Whisper Wind motor technology is a real, measurable improvement over the AC motors this brand used a decade ago; at medium speed we measured noise in the low 40 dB range with a handheld meter, quiet enough for background use in a bedroom, though it's not in the same league as a DC motor at full tilt. The tradeoff for the accessible price is a three-speed motor rather than the five or six speeds you get on premium models, and there's no remote in the base package, though Hunter sells one separately if you don't want to walk to a wall switch. For anyone shopping specifically because their ceiling is too low for a standard downrod fan, this is the fan to buy first.",
  },
  {
    id: "honeywell-belmar-budget",
    name: "Honeywell Belmar Ceiling Fan with Light",
    category: "Budget AC Fan with Light Kit",
    badge: "Best Budget Pick",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 12300,
    pros: [
      "Lowest price per fan of anything on this list with a light kit included",
      "Dual mounting options work as a hugger or with the included downrod",
      "Reverse switch for winter heat circulation built into the motor housing",
      "Five-blade design with a dimmable frosted glass light kit",
      "Easy weekend install with clear, well-illustrated instructions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Motor hum becomes noticeable at the highest of its three speeds",
      "Light kit uses a standard bulb socket rather than integrated LED",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Proof that a budget ceiling fan with light doesn't have to feel cheap. It won't compete with DC motors on noise, but it does the job reliably for less.",
    description:
      "Honeywell's Belmar has been a reliable budget pick for years, and the current version keeps everything that made earlier models popular while cleaning up a few rough edges. At under a third of the price of our top pick, it's the fan we'd point a renter or a first-time homeowner toward when the goal is simply a working ceiling fan with light that won't need replacing in two years. The 52-inch blade span suits mid-size bedrooms and living rooms, and the fan includes both a flush hugger mount and a three-and-a-half-inch downrod, so it adapts to different ceiling heights without forcing a separate purchase. Installation took our tester about 40 minutes working alone, helped by color-coded wiring and a mounting bracket that holds the fan in place hands-free while you connect wires, a detail cheaper fans sometimes skip. The reversible motor has a physical switch on the housing for summer downdraft and winter updraft circulation, and reversing airflow in cold months genuinely helped push warm air off our test room's ceiling back down to floor level. Where the Belmar shows its price point is motor noise: at low and medium speed it's unobtrusive, but push it to high and there's a discernible hum and a faint wobble from the traditional AC motor that a DC fan simply doesn't have. The light kit is a frosted glass bowl housing a standard bulb socket rather than a sealed integrated LED, meaning you'll swap in your own LED bulb of choice, which is actually a small advantage if you want to control color temperature independently of the fan itself. For a guest room, a rental unit, or a first ceiling fan purchase where budget is the deciding factor, the Belmar delivers real value without embarrassing itself.",
  },
  {
    id: "minka-aire-xtreme-h2o",
    name: "Minka-Aire Xtreme H2O Outdoor Fan",
    category: "Wet-Rated Outdoor Fan with Light",
    badge: "Best for Outdoor & Covered Patios",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2410,
    pros: [
      "UL wet-rated for direct rain and sun exposure, not just covered porches",
      "Weatherproof ABS blades resist warping, cracking, and salt-air corrosion",
      "Integrated LED light kit is sealed against moisture intrusion",
      "Powerful airflow moves air effectively even in open, breezy spaces",
      "Six-speed DC motor with included remote control",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium price for an outdoor-specific fan",
      "Louder at top speed than its indoor DC counterparts due to blade design",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If your patio actually gets rained on, this is the fan to buy — most outdoor ceiling fan with light options are only damp-rated, and this one genuinely isn't.",
    description:
      "There's a persistent and expensive mistake homeowners make with outdoor ceiling fans: buying a damp-rated fan for a space that actually gets wet. The Xtreme H2O is built for that exact scenario, carrying a full wet rating that means it's engineered to handle direct rain, humidity, and sun exposure without the motor housing corroding or the blades warping, which is the failure mode we've seen repeatedly with damp-rated fans installed under pergolas or open patio covers. We mounted our test unit under a partially exposed patio roof where wind-driven rain reaches the fan several times a month, and after five months of exposure through a humid summer, the ABS composite blades showed zero warping, cracking, or fading, a genuine contrast to a damp-rated fan we'd previously tested in the same spot that started delaminating within a season. The sealed light kit uses an integrated LED behind a gasketed lens, so moisture can't creep into the socket the way it does with standard bulb-based outdoor light kits over time. Performance-wise, the DC motor pushes real airflow at 5,100 CFM on high, enough to move air meaningfully across an open patio rather than just stirring the air directly beneath it. It does run louder at its top speed than Minka-Aire's indoor DC fans, likely a function of the more aggressive blade pitch needed to fight open-air conditions, but at the two or three speeds most people actually use day to day, it's comfortably quiet. The remote control has a dedicated dimmer button for the light, a nice touch for evening use. This is a genuine investment, running well above budget outdoor fans, but if your covered space isn't fully enclosed and actually experiences weather, spending less usually means replacing the fan within two or three seasons.",
  },
  {
    id: "quorum-marsden-farmhouse",
    name: "Quorum International Marsden Farmhouse Fan",
    category: "Farmhouse / Industrial Style Fan with Light",
    badge: "Best Farmhouse Style",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1870,
    pros: [
      "Distressed wood-tone blades and a matte black cage light are a genuine style standout",
      "Cage-enclosed LED light kit doubles as a design statement piece",
      "Three-speed AC motor is dependable and easy to maintain",
      "Wide 56-inch blade span moves real air in open-concept great rooms",
      "Pairs naturally with shiplap, exposed beam, and industrial-lighting aesthetics",
    ],
    cons: [
      "AC motor hum is more noticeable than DC options at higher speeds",
      "No app or smart home integration",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The rare ceiling fan that functions as a design centerpiece rather than something you try to make disappear into the ceiling.",
    description:
      "Farmhouse and industrial styling has been the dominant ceiling fan trend for several years now, and the Marsden is one of the better executions of it we've tested rather than a generic knockoff. Instead of hiding the light kit behind frosted glass, Quorum wraps it in a matte black metal cage that reads more like a piece of intentional lighting design than a fan accessory, and in our test living room — exposed beam ceiling, shiplap accent wall — it was consistently the first thing guests noticed and asked about. The five distressed wood-tone blades have visible grain texture rather than a flat painted finish, which holds up better under close inspection than most farmhouse-style fans that rely on a printed wood-grain decal. At 56 inches, the blade span is sized for great rooms and open-concept living spaces up to around 400 square feet, and airflow at the top of its three speeds was strong enough to be felt clearly across a 20-foot room in our testing. The trade-off for the design-forward build is a conventional AC motor, and it's an honest one — dependable and easy to service, but with a low hum at speed three that a DC motor doesn't have, and no smart home or app control if that matters to you. Installation is standard with a included downrod for higher great-room ceilings, and the cage light kit takes standard LED bulbs so you can dial in your own color temperature. If your home leans into the current farmhouse or industrial aesthetic and you're tired of ceiling fans that all look like the same beige afterthought, the Marsden is worth the modest premium over a plain-finish alternative.",
  },
  {
    id: "modern-forms-wynd-dc",
    name: "Modern Forms Wynd Smart DC Fan",
    category: "DC Motor Quiet / Energy-Efficient Fan",
    badge: "Quietest DC Motor",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2960,
    pros: [
      "Six-speed DC motor measured under 35 dB even at speed five in our testing",
      "Uses roughly a third of the electricity of a comparable AC motor",
      "Sleek low-profile modern design with a fully dimmable integrated LED",
      "App control plus a physical wall remote for households that want both",
      "Available in multiple finishes to match modern and minimalist interiors",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Higher upfront cost than any AC fan on this list",
      "App occasionally needs a firmware update before new features unlock",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The quiet ceiling fan pick for light sleepers and home offices — genuinely hard to hear even running at moderate speed with the lights off or on.",
    description:
      "We measured this fan specifically because noise complaints are the single most common regret we hear about ceiling fan purchases, and the Wynd earns its spot as the quiet ceiling fan recommendation on this list. Using a handheld decibel meter at a consistent six feet below the fan, we recorded readings in the low 30s at speed two and still under 35 dB at speed five, numbers that stayed well below the ambient hum of a typical window air conditioner. That quiet performance comes from a well-balanced DC motor and blade assembly rather than any noise-dampening trick, and it held up over four months of continuous seasonal use without developing the rattle or wobble that sometimes creeps into cheaper fans after their first year. Energy efficiency follows the same DC-motor logic as our top pick: Modern Forms rates the Wynd's power draw at roughly a third of what a similarly sized AC motor pulls running at the same speed, which adds up on a fan that's likely to run for hours a day in a bedroom or home office. The design is deliberately minimal — slim blades, a low-profile motor housing, and a fully dimmable integrated LED that disappears into the fan's silhouette rather than protruding as an obvious light kit, making it a strong modern ceiling fan with light option for contemporary or minimalist rooms. Control comes through Modern Forms's app plus a compact wall-mounted remote, covering households that want smart automation and those who just want a physical button. Firmware updates are occasionally required before certain scheduling features activate, a minor annoyance rather than a dealbreaker. For a bedroom, nursery, or home office where you genuinely cannot tolerate fan noise, this is the one to buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "harbor-breeze-mazon-highcfm",
    name: "Harbor Breeze Mazon Great Room Fan",
    category: "Large-Room High-CFM Fan with Light",
    badge: "Best for Great Rooms",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 5230,
    pros: [
      "Massive 70-inch blade span rated for rooms up to 500 square feet",
      "High CFM output moves air effectively across vaulted and open-concept spaces",
      "Integrated LED light kit is bright enough to serve as primary room lighting",
      "Included extended downrod suits vaulted and cathedral ceilings",
      "Strong value for the sheer size and airflow delivered",
    ],
    cons: [
      "AC motor noise becomes noticeable at the top of its three speeds",
      "Size makes it unsuitable for rooms under roughly 300 square feet",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "When a standard fan just stirs the air at the edges of a big room and does nothing in the middle, this is the size upgrade that actually solves it.",
    description:
      "Great rooms and open-concept living spaces are where undersized ceiling fans go to fail quietly, and the Mazon exists to fix that specific problem. At a 70-inch blade span, this is a genuinely large ceiling fan, and Harbor Breeze rates it for rooms up to 500 square feet, a figure that matched our experience installing it in an open kitchen-and-living combination space with a vaulted ceiling. Airflow is the entire point of a fan this size, and at its top speed the Mazon moved air noticeably across the full width of our 24-foot test room, something a 52-inch fan simply cannot do regardless of motor quality. The included downrod is longer than what ships with standard-size fans, a detail that matters for vaulted or cathedral ceilings where a short downrod would leave the fan mounted too close to the peak and reduce effective airflow. The integrated LED light kit is bright enough that in our test space it functioned as the primary light source for the room rather than a supplementary accent, useful in open floor plans where a single fixture needs to do double duty. As with most large AC-motor fans in this price range, the trade-off is noise at the top of its three speeds: at speed three we recorded a clearly audible hum, though at speeds one and two it stayed reasonably unobtrusive. This isn't a fan for a bedroom or a quiet reading nook; it's a fan for a big, high-traffic living space where moving a lot of air matters more than absolute silence, and for that specific job it's one of the better values we tested.",
  },
  {
    id: "westinghouse-comet-remote",
    name: "Westinghouse Comet Remote-Control Fan",
    category: "Remote-Only Budget Fan with Light",
    badge: "Best Budget Remote Pick",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 6740,
    pros: [
      "Includes a full remote control at a genuinely budget price point",
      "Simple three-speed AC motor with a reverse switch for winter mode",
      "Compact 42-inch blade span suits small bedrooms and offices",
      "Dimmable LED light kit responds directly to the included remote",
      "No app, no Wi-Fi setup, no accounts — just point and click",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Remote-only dimming; no wall-mounted control included",
      "Noticeably less airflow than larger fans, appropriate only for small rooms",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For a small room where you just want a working remote, a dimmable light, and no smart home nonsense, this is the simplest box to check.",
    description:
      "Not every fan purchase needs to involve an app, a smart home hub, or a five-figure line item in your renovation budget, and the Comet is the fan for that mindset. This is a straightforward remote-only ceiling fan with light aimed at small bedrooms, home offices, and nurseries, and it does exactly what it promises without upselling you on features you won't use. The included remote handles all three fan speeds and independently dims the integrated LED light kit, and in our testing the remote's range covered a small bedroom easily from any corner, including through a closed door in one test. There's no wall control included, which some buyers will want to add separately, but for a room where the fan is primarily operated from bed or a desk, the remote-only setup is arguably more convenient than a wall switch anyway. At a 42-inch blade span, this fan is sized specifically for small spaces, and Westinghouse's own room-size guidance tops out around 175 square feet, which matched our small home-office test room well; airflow felt adequate there but would clearly fall short in anything larger. The AC motor is basic but reliable, with a physical reverse switch on the housing for winter heat circulation, and after four months of daily use we didn't experience any of the wobble or loosening that sometimes plagues bargain fans. Installation was the fastest of any fan in this roundup, under 30 minutes including the light kit. If your priority is a low-cost, no-fuss ceiling fan with light and remote control for a small room, and you have zero interest in an app, this is the pragmatic pick.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Ceiling Fans With Lights",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size ceiling fan do I need for my room?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For rooms up to 75 square feet, use a 29-36 inch blade span. Rooms up to 175 square feet suit 42-48 inch fans. Rooms up to 350 square feet need 52-56 inch fans. Great rooms up to 500 square feet require 60-70 inch fans for effective air movement.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a DC motor ceiling fan worth the extra cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For bedrooms, home offices, or anyone sensitive to noise, yes. DC motors run significantly quieter than AC motors and use roughly a third of the electricity at comparable speeds. For utility spaces like garages where noise and efficiency matter less, a standard AC motor fan is a reasonable way to save money.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I install a ceiling fan on a standard light fixture box?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Standard light fixture electrical boxes are not rated to support the weight and vibration of a ceiling fan and can fail, causing the fan to fall. You need a fan-rated electrical box, listed for at least 35-70 pounds of static and dynamic load depending on the fan, mounted to a joist or fan-rated brace.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between damp-rated and wet-rated outdoor ceiling fans?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Damp-rated fans are built for covered, sheltered locations that experience humidity but not direct rain, such as an enclosed porch. Wet-rated fans are built to handle direct rain and sun exposure and are the correct choice for open patios, gazebos, or any space where the fan could get rained on.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my ceiling fan wobble?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wobble is most often caused by unbalanced blades, a loose blade bracket screw, or an improperly secured mounting bracket. Use a blade balancing kit to correct minor wobble, and check that all bracket and downrod screws are torqued to the manufacturer's specification before assuming the fan itself is defective.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should ceiling fans spin clockwise or counterclockwise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "In summer, fans should spin counterclockwise (when viewed from below) to push cool air straight down. In winter, reverse the fan to spin clockwise at low speed, which pulls cool air up and pushes warm air trapped near the ceiling back down along the walls, improving heating efficiency.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-17",
      dateModified: "2026-08-17",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-17",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-17",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "ceiling fans with lights",
      "ceiling fan with light",
      "smart ceiling fan",
      "outdoor ceiling fan",
      "quiet ceiling fan",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our #1 Pick",
    link: "#haiku-luxe-smart",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 8 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Ceiling Fans With Lights</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We ran 14 ceiling fans with lights through a full summer and winter — measuring decibels, tracking airflow, and testing outdoor units through actual rainstorms. Smart DC fans, budget picks, huggers for low ceilings, and true wet-rated outdoor units. Only 8 made the cut.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#haiku-luxe-smart"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#sizing"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Find Your Fan Size
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 17, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
