        <!-- SECTION 1: Sizing -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Size Ceiling Fan Do You Need?
          </h2>
          <p>
            The single most common mistake in ceiling fan shopping is buying based on looks alone and ignoring room size. An undersized fan in a large room will spin at full speed and barely move air past the first few feet below it. An oversized fan crammed into a small bedroom is not just visually wrong — it can feel like standing in a wind tunnel and will drown out a bedside conversation. Blade span (measured tip to tip across the diameter) should scale directly with your room's square footage.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room Size</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recommended Blade Span</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example Room</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Up to 75 sq ft</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">29-36 inches</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Small bathroom, laundry room, walk-in closet</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
