                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">42-48 inches</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Small bedroom, home office, nursery</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">176-350 sq ft</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52-56 inches</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Master bedroom, standard living room</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">351-450 sq ft</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">56-60 inches</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Large living room, den</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">450+ sq ft</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-70 inches</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Great room, open-concept living space</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Blade span is only half the equation. The other number that matters — and one most shoppers never check — is CFM, or cubic feet per minute, which tells you how much air the fan actually moves at its highest speed. Two fans with identical 52-inch blade spans can have meaningfully different CFM ratings depending on blade pitch, motor torque, and blade count. As a rough benchmark, look for at least 4,000-5,000 CFM in a mid-size bedroom or living room, and 6,000+ CFM for a great room or open-concept space. A fan with a wide blade span but a weak motor will look right on paper and still underperform in the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling height also affects sizing decisions. Standard 8-foot ceilings need the blades mounted at least 7 feet off the floor and at least 10-12 inches below the ceiling for proper airflow, which usually means a hugger or flush-mount fan rather than one on a long downrod. Vaulted or cathedral ceilings need the opposite treatment — an extended downrod to bring the fan down closer to the ideal 8-9 foot mounting height, since a fan mounted too close to a high peak barely moves air in the living space below it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Light Kit Type: Integrated LED vs. Add-On Kit</h3>
          <p>
            Sizing isn't only about the blades — the light kit deserves its own set of questions before you buy. Most modern ceiling fans with lights ship one of two ways: an integrated LED module built directly into the motor housing, or a separate add-on light kit that bolts to the bottom of the fan and takes a standard bulb socket. Integrated LEDs are typically brighter for their size, run cooler, dim more smoothly, and rarely need replacement over the fan's lifespan since the diodes are rated for tens of thousands of hours. Add-on kits with standard sockets cost less to manufacture, but they let you choose your own bulb, which matters if you have strong preferences about color temperature or want to swap in a smart bulb independent of the fan's own controls.
          </p>
          <p>
            Color temperature is worth checking on the spec sheet before you buy, not after. Warm light in the 2700-3000K range suits bedrooms and living rooms and reads close to a traditional incandescent bulb. Cooler light in the 4000-5000K range is better suited to kitchens, home offices, and workshops where task visibility matters more than ambiance. Some premium fans, including the app-connected models in this guide, let you adjust color temperature on the fly rather than locking you into one setting, which is a genuinely useful feature if the same room serves multiple purposes throughout the day. Dimmability is the other detail to confirm: not every fan light kit dims smoothly, and cheaper LED drivers can flicker or step unevenly at low brightness rather than fading cleanly.
          </p>

