        <!-- SECTION 2: DC vs AC -->
        <div id="dc-vs-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. DC vs. AC Motor: What Actually Matters
          </h2>
          <p>
            This is the single biggest performance and price differentiator among modern ceiling fans, and it's the one variable that most shoppers browsing by looks alone completely skip. Understanding it will save you from either overspending on a feature you don't need, or underspending and regretting the noise every single night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AC (Alternating Current) Motors</h3>
          <p>
            The traditional ceiling fan motor, found in the vast majority of budget and mid-range fans. AC motors are simple, durable, and inexpensive to manufacture, which keeps fan prices down. The trade-off is efficiency and noise: AC motors typically offer just three fixed speeds, draw noticeably more electricity to produce the same airflow as a DC motor, and tend to produce an audible hum or whir, especially at their highest speed. For a garage, a utility room, or any budget-conscious purchase where noise isn't a priority, a well-built AC fan is a completely reasonable choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DC (Direct Current) Motors</h3>
          <p>
            DC motors use a permanent-magnet design that's dramatically more energy-efficient — typically drawing a third or less of the electricity an AC motor needs to move the same amount of air — while running significantly quieter, often by a wide margin at comparable speeds. They also typically offer six or more speed settings instead of three, giving far more granular control over airflow. The trade-off is upfront cost: DC-motor fans generally cost noticeably more than a comparable AC fan. For bedrooms, home offices, nurseries, or anyone who runs their fan for hours a day, the extra cost is usually recovered over time through lower electricity use, on top of the comfort benefit of a quiet ceiling fan you can actually sleep under.
          </p>

